      "Every normalized ideal envelope on a curve is the ideal projection of a Green potential with its atomic data. Divisorial domination and the envelope lower bound give ordinary equivalence, including exhausted-area zero mass.",
      "The common polar part of a maximum is the coefficientwise minimum. The cited dimension-one d_S inequality has constant48, giving total-variation bounds1/96 and1/2.",
      "Atomic measures of mass at most area-epsilon are complete in total variation because the countable union of supports contains the limit. Rational finite truncations from below preserve the mass bound and give analytic density.",
      "Point masses at distinct points have fixed total-variation separation and therefore show failure of total boundedness; this is compatible with the separate totally bounded canonical L1 metric.",
      "Normalized restricted approximants have a common curvature bound and converge after extraction to a genuine potential in the limiting background. Each fixed modification preserves the already chosen L1 subsequence.",
      "Lelong semicontinuity on each modification gives every trace lower bound. It does not by itself give exactness; no simultaneous extraction over all divisors is used.",
      "The trace family is stable under finite maxima. Choquet approximation, pullback convergence and semicontinuity put the regularized supremum back into its defining family.",
      "A finite log resolution of each restricted ideal makes every trace competitor ordinarily dominate that analytic restriction. This gives a common lower barrier for the ordinary/ideal analytic envelopes.",
      "Every cluster limit retains trace constraints and limiting background and is therefore the maximal trace envelope. Compactness yields full L1 convergence. Dyadic ideal inclusion and decreasing backgrounds yield pointwise decrease.",
      "Ideal projection retains every divisorial constraint, so maximality gives ideal fixedness; ordinary fixedness follows from the retained ordinary-preserves-I result.",
      "Pulled-back backgrounds force descent under modifications. A common smooth refinement preserves each generic prime-divisor order and identifies the inherited trace competitor families.",
      "Decreasing positive perturbations on the modification stay between the pulled-back trace and zero; the fixed integrable lower barrier gives pointwise and L1 convergence, independent of perturbing metric.",
      "In a sufficiently large closed Kahler target background the majorant in the half background supplies positive mass. Exact divisorial convergence then uses the cited SGPT decreasing-model theorem.",
      "Larger Hermitian backgrounds contain that exact closed-background representative and squeeze all trace numbers to equality. Without the retained BMP or special geometry hypotheses, the general non-Kahler result still gives only lower bounds.",
      "Doubling a background admitting a trace majorant gives a genuine half-background obstacle competitor, both model fixed-point properties and positive finite mass by the retained contact and individual-positivity inputs.",
      "The prescribed ordinary-type equation theorem applies only after both model-condition clauses have been checked; the corollary states uniqueness of the scalar in the constant-density-factor equation.",
      "For a connected-fiber curve submersion, multiplier ideals pull back exactly and restricted curve ideals have a single local generator; vertical valuations multiply by the fiber multiplicity and horizontal valuations vanish.",
      "At a general vertical divisor a root of the local unit puts the map in the form z1^k. Circular means give exact pullback Lelong number. A horizontal divisor has an open image containing finite Green values and hence generic Lelong number zero.",
      "The countable fixed-fiber divisorial potential identifies trace constraints with ordinary domination. Ideal projection and the same domination lemma show ordinary and ideal envelope equality in every sufficiently large background.",
      "The curve-fibration trace has positive finite mass and prescribed-type equations in the doubled background. Green changes are smooth and only ordinary type, rather than equality of normalized envelopes, is claimed background independent.",
      "The bounded product formula leaves precisely one curve factor and k transverse factors, with binomial coefficient k+1. Local smooth corrections and Bedford--Taylor limits justify the exterior-product identity for bounded potentials.",
      "For arbitrary potentials the increasing plurifine sets {w>-j} x {h>-j} exhaust the finite locus; sigma-finiteness and non-pluripolarity remove the complementary poles, so the product identity includes infinite total mass.",
      "A Green trace with positive residual area has smooth positive finite product measure. Separated Lp densities use the curve Poisson solution and the retained bounded Hermitian equation, and Fubini preserves Lp.",
      "An everywhere exponentially integrable transverse infinite-mass witness preserves every multiplier ideal by local generators and Fubini. Positive curve residual mass makes the product mass infinite while all exact divisorial trace numbers remain unchanged; hence the product fails BMP.",
      "The final resolved-restriction mass formula retains a closed background, finite divisorial part and bounded remainder on a Kahler model. The ideal trace data alone does not provide those hypotheses."
